---
title: Getting started
description: Create an account, enter the dashboard, and run your first match.
---

# Getting started

This guide takes a new participant from an invitation to an active driver station. A lobby owner can complete the same flow and then follow the [administration guide](/docs/administration).

## Access and account

Select **Request Invite** on the [home page](/) if you need an invitation. Include your name, team or organization, and email so Team Vietnam can provision access.

When you receive a code, open [Sign in](/auth), choose **Register**, and provide your name, team, email, password, and invitation code. Codes are single-use and should be kept private until registration is complete. Successful registration opens an authenticated session and returns you to the page you originally requested when possible.

On a shared computer, use **Sign out** from a protected page when you finish. If a code fails, check each character and ask its issuer for a new code; redeemed, revoked, and expired codes cannot be reused.

## Start a match

Open the [dashboard](/dashboard) and choose **Create New Match**. The creator becomes the room host. Share the lobby with participants, then use the lobby to select one of the pack's eight stations:

| Alliance | Driver stations         | Human-player station |
| -------- | ----------------------- | -------------------- |
| Red      | Red Driver 1, 2, and 3  | Red Human            |
| Blue     | Blue Driver 1, 2, and 3 | Blue Human           |

Assign the robot revision before the host locks the room. Once locked, the station claim and selected revision are frozen into the match bootstrap so every player, spectator, and server runs the same match specification.

Drivers mark themselves ready. The host starts only when the roster is ready; the service then issues time-limited station tickets and launches the match's countdown.

## Join and rejoin

Open the lobby link, select the station or spectator role that the host has assigned, and obtain a ticket. A driver ticket is bound to its match, alliance, role, and station. It cannot be reused to take another station or send control input as a different participant.

If the browser disconnects, reopen the same lobby and rejoin with a fresh ticket. The authoritative match continues while the client is away. On reconnect, the server supplies a full current baseline before incremental snapshots resume, so balls, robots, score, and clock return together.

> A driver can send drive and mechanism frames. Spectators receive snapshots and match events only. Host and reviewer capabilities are explicit and never implied by a spectator connection.
